  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA weak cold front will remain stalled across the forecast area\ntoday. After early morning fog (some possibly locally dense) \nand a few showers, we'll expect variable clouds. In addition \nthe weak front should provide modest convergence to allow for \nwidely scattered convective showers and isolated thunderstorms \nto develop. Nothing strong or severe expected. Dewpoint values \nwill be up there though, making it feel quite muggy.\n\nThe return of fog is expected again tonight, some of it possibly\nlocally dense as the moist airmass remains stagnant. \n\nWednesday will be a warmer day and feature more sunshine as\nwell. It will also be drier in terms of mugginess. A cold front\nwill approach from the west late in the day. However, timing\nseems such that it will be too late to deliver any sort of \nthunderstorm activity.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nIf the front continues to slow as the parent low occludes east \nof Hudson Bay, then it may wind up draped across the forecast \narea or just to the east. This would be the eventual pathway for\nlow pressure across the Ohio Valley to track into the latter \nhalf of the week. Thus precip chances Thursday and Friday could \neventually be in flux.\n\nHigh pressure should be nearing the region come the weekend, but\nthere will be the complicating factor of the slow moving \nboundary exiting, mentioned above, and a potential cut off low \nor trough arriving from the west. The combination of the low and\nreturn flow could spell for a damp period across much of the\nNortheast, but anticipate some slowing of the low's transit \neast which could help the first half of the weekend remain dry.",
